---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro';
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import JsonLd from '../components/JsonLd.astro';
import { AUTHOR, SOCIAL_LINKS, RESUME_PDF_PATH } from '../consts';

const site = Astro.site!;

const personSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Person',
  name: AUTHOR.name,
  url: new URL('/about/', site).href,
  jobTitle: AUTHOR.jobTitle,
  email: `mailto:${AUTHOR.email}`,
  sameAs: SOCIAL_LINKS.filter((l) => l.href.startsWith('http')).map((l) => l.href),
};
---

<PageLayout
  title="About"
  eyebrow="Background"
  heading="About me"
  lede="Where I've been, what I like building, and how I work."
  description="Background, engineering philosophy and working style — Vince Caldwell, senior software engineer."
  narrow
>
  <JsonLd schema={personSchema} slot="head" />

  <Prose>
    <p>
      I'm a senior software engineer in Jupiter, Florida, currently at
      PoliteMail. Most of what I've built over the last few years sits at the
      seam between a product's front end and the distributed system holding it
      up — onboarding and licensing flows, a secure mail delivery service on
      Azure, and a long-running effort to modernize a legacy codebase without
      stopping shipping.
    </p>

    <h2>How I got here</h2>
    <p>
      My degree is in business management and finance, not computer science. I
      came into engineering through the work itself, and I think it left me
      better at the part a lot of engineers skip: figuring out what a
      requirement actually means before writing anything. I started at Genesis
      Global Technologies integrating platforms with APIs like HubSpot and
      Zapier, joined PoliteMail in 2022, and moved into the senior role in 2023.
    </p>

    <h2>What I'm good at</h2>
    <p>
      Two things, mostly. Designing backend systems that behave under real
      conditions — Azure Durable Functions, REST APIs, Microsoft Graph, and the
      authentication, token management, retry and throttling work that decides
      whether any of it survives contact with production. And modernizing front
      ends without a rewrite: moving legacy React to hooks and functional
      patterns while the product keeps shipping.
    </p>

    <h2>How I work</h2>
    <p>
      I write the documentation people actually use — onboarding guides at
      PoliteMail cut new-developer ramp-up time by about 40%, which I'm prouder
      of than most of the code. I review carefully, mentor the engineers around
      me, and spend real time on pipelines and developer experience, because
      friction there is a tax the whole team pays every day.
    </p>
    <p>
      I lean on tools like Copilot and Claude Code for the mechanical parts, and
      I'm deliberate about where they help and where they don't. They buy time
      for architecture decisions; they don't make those decisions.
    </p>

    <h2>What I'm looking for</h2>
    <p>
      Senior engineering work on products with real systems behind them —
      distributed backends, thoughtful APIs, and teams that treat maintainability
      as a feature. Happy to talk either way; the
      <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> has the fastest way to reach me.
    </p>
  </Prose>

  <div class="mt-12 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
    <Button href="/resume/">
      Read my resume
      <Icon name="arrow-right" size={16} />
    </Button>
    <Button href={RESUME_PDF_PATH} variant="ghost" download>
      Download PDF
      <Icon name="download" size={16} />
    </Button>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
